Initialise: pick k points as starting centroids. Assign: each point → its nearest centroid (Euclidean distance √Σ(aᵢ−bᵢ)²). Update: each centroid → the mean of its assigned points. Repeat 2–3 until centroids stop moving ( convergence ). Those two alternating steps are the whole algorithm, and each one is provably optimal given the other. Holding the centroids fixed, assigning each point to its nearest centroid is the best possible assignment. Holding the assignment fixed, the mean is the point that minimises the sum of squared distances to a group. Alternating two locally optimal steps is why K-means always converges — and why it converges to a local minimum rather than the global one. What it minimises SSE (inertia) = Σ over clusters  Σ over points  ‖x − centroid‖²

the assign step minimises SSE over assignments, centroids fixed
the update step minimises SSE over centroids, assignments fixed
→ SSE falls every iteration, so the loop must terminate Weaknesses to know: you must choose k ; the result depends on the initialisation (hence the fixed seed); it assumes roughly round, similar-sized clusters; and it is sensitive to feature scale. The "round clusters" assumption follows directly from the use of Euclidean distance and a mean. A cluster is defined as "everything closer to this centroid than to any other", and that region is always a convex polygon. Two interleaved crescents cannot be separated by it, however many times you run the loop. The elbow method Run k = 1, 2, 3, …, plot SSE vs k. SSE always falls as k rises (k = n points gives SSE 0), so look for the "elbow" , where adding clusters stops paying off. k SSE Drop from previous 1 492.6 — 2 86.3 406.3 3 41.0 45.3 4 27.5 13.5 Study guide Q27. The huge drop at k=2, then diminishing returns → elbow at k=2, which also matches the question's "two classes". Read the third column rather than the second. Going from 1 to 2 clusters removes 406 of the 493 available SSE — 82% of it. Going from 2 to 3 removes another 45, and from 3 to 4 only 13. The curve has a corner at 2 because the data genuinely has two groups; after that you are subdividing groups that were already coherent. SSE always falls, so the number itself tells you nothing — only its bend does. At k = n every point is its own cluster and SSE is exactly zero. Any method that picks k by minimising SSE will therefore pick k = n. The six functions, as the rubric wants them Q Function Logic Q22 closest_centroid(point, centroids) np.argmin(np.linalg.norm(centroids - point, axis=1)) Q23 compute_centroids(clusters) np.mean(points_in_cluster, axis=0) for each cluster id → NumPy array. An empty cluster keeps its old centroid (avoids NaN) Q24 initialize_centroids(seed, p) np.random.seed(seed) ; points[np.random.choice(len(points), p, replace=False)] . replace=False prevents duplicate starting centroids Q25 compute_sse(centroids, clusters, k) sum of squared distances from each point to its own centroid Q26 kmeans(p, data_points, centroids) loop: assign → update → stop when np.allclose(new, old, atol=1e-6) . Returns (centroids, clusters_dict) Q27–28 the elbow, then k = 2 loop k = 1..4 with seed 42, print every SSE, plot SSE vs k; then scatter the k=2 clusters in two colours with centroids as red X  Specific marks: Q24's replace=False stops two centroids starting on the same point. Q26 must stop on a tolerance — the rubric says "above a small tolerance", so np.allclose(new, old, atol=1e-6) , never == on floats. Q27 awards marks for printing every SSE, not just plotting it, and it must use your kmeans from Q26. Q23's empty-cluster guard matters: without it a cluster that loses all its points produces a NaN centroid and poisons the rest of the run. the six functions import numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt

def closest_centroid(point, centroids):
    return int(np.argmin(np.linalg.norm(centroids - point, axis=1)))

def assign(points, centroids):
    clusters = {i: [] for i in range(len(centroids))}
    for pt in points:
        clusters[closest_centroid(pt, centroids)].append(pt)
    return clusters

def compute_centroids(clusters, old):
    out = []
    for i in sorted(clusters):
        pts = clusters[i]
        out.append(np.mean(pts, axis=0) if len(pts) else old[i])   # empty -> keep old
    return np.array(out)

def initialize_centroids(seed, p, points):
    np.random.seed(seed)
    return points[np.random.choice(len(points), p, replace=False)]

def compute_sse(centroids, clusters, k):
    return sum(float(np.sum((np.array(clusters[i]) - centroids[i]) ** 2))
               for i in range(k) if len(clusters[i]))

def kmeans(p, data_points, centroids, atol=1e-6, max_iter=300):
    for _ in range(max_iter):
        clusters = assign(data_points, centroids)
        new = compute_centroids(clusters, centroids)
        if np.allclose(new, centroids, atol=atol):      # tolerance, not ==
            centroids = new
            break
        centroids = new
    return centroids, clusters

# Q27: the elbow, printing every SSE as the rubric requires
sse = []
for k in range(1, 5):
    c0 = initialize_centroids(42, k, data_points)
    c, cl = kmeans(k, data_points, c0)
    s = compute_sse(c, cl, k)
    print(f"k={k}  SSE={s:.1f}")                        # PRINT, not just plot
    sse.append(s)

plt.plot(range(1, 5), sse, marker="o")
plt.xlabel("k"); plt.ylabel("SSE"); plt.title("Elbow method"); plt.show() `compute_centroids` takes the old centroids so an emptied cluster can keep its position instead of becoming NaN — the guard the rubric looks for.
